      "category": "short_to_ground",
      "components": ["D1", "C4", "C5", "C6", "C7", "J5", "J8"],
      "pins": ["D1.2", "C4.1", "C5.1", "C6.1", "C7.1"],
      "nets": ["servo power rail", "GND"],
      "observed": "The node after D1 connects to the servo power rail and continues down to a GND symbol. The positive terminals of the servo-rail capacitors are on this same grounded node.",
      "expected": "The servo power rail should be supplied from +5V through D1 and remain separate from GND; the capacitor positive terminals should be on that rail, with their negative terminals at GND.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "schematic",
          "quote": "\"D1 PMEG3030EP\"; \"C7 100u\"; \"C4 100u\"; \"GND\" (sheet /, page 1; the drawn wires join the D1 output/servo rail and capacitor positive terminals to GND)"
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "“From +5V, a second diode D2 (PMEG3030EP, annotated ‘3A 360mV’ in the schematic — a low-forward-voltage-drop Schottky) feeds a dedicated servo power rail backed by four 100 µF capacitors (C1–C4) before reaching J3/J8 pin 2.”"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "The servo supply is shorted to ground, so the servos cannot receive power. If D1 is forward-biased from +5V toward the rail, this also creates a path that can short the +5V supply through D1.",
      "confidence": 0.94
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The schematic connects the D1-fed servo power rail directly to GND. This contradicts the specified diode-fed servo supply and would prevent normal servo operation."
}

